From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Subtitles: translate subtitles made of pictures, read by the
 AI provider's model

Only text could be translated, so a Blu-ray, a DVD or a DVB recording
with picture subtitles in another language got nothing. Such a track,
chosen to be read in another language, now opens in a codec of its own:
the player's own decoder turns its packets into pictures, and the AI
provider's model reads them as text - when it reads images - which then
goes the way a text subtitle's does: repaired, translated, timed and
shown as text.

Text read off a picture carries no tags: every character of it is the
subtitle's own - some tracks open a narrator's line with "< " - so the
repair reads none of it as markup, the translation sends it as it is,
and the display skips the tag conversion a text track's cues go through
and writes the text so libass shows each character as itself: a brace
escaped, a backslash with a word joiner after it, so it starts none of
libass's own ("\N", "\h", "\{").

Each line is shown where its picture was, by the third of the video its
centre is in - one eye's video for a 3D subtitle: a sign at the top
stays at the top, a line at a speaker's side stays at that side. A line
at the bottom centre, as most are, is placed as any text subtitle is.

Each picture is drawn in grey from its palette, the text's light as
opaque as it is - its dark outline is not ink - and cropped to the text;
a 3D subtitle is read by one eye - the player's split for a 3D screen,
or cut from the picture of both on a 2D one - and the space between
lines far apart closes to a line's. Its lines are counted and it is
scaled so each is about 32 pixels high: the text's own height decides,
not the video's, so a DVD subtitle keeps its size and a 4K one shrinks a
lot, and none is enlarged. Eight are stacked in one picture, numbered,
for one request - a model writes their text at the same pace however
many, so the request costs what its answer does: a little under a second
a subtitle on an 8 GB Jetson running Gemma 4 E4B, which read 79 of 80
Blu-ray subtitles exactly. The player reads a disc's subtitles only
about ten seconds ahead of the picture - three after a seek - and
reading and translating a line takes about four, so the track is also
read a minute ahead of playback in a demuxer of its own: the video
opened again as playback opens it, a disc's playlist through the disc so
both count time from the same start, a file without the player's cache,
at no more than four times playback's pace so it takes nothing from it.
Its subtitles and the player's are read once, whichever comes first; one
the player reads where the read-ahead has been without seeing it stops
the read-ahead, and the player's packets carry on alone. A picture is
handed on as soon as it is read, not with the next packet, which can
come minutes later; a clear goes the same way, in order. The display
ends each line as the player ends a picture: where the next starts or
clears, or at its own end when that comes first - PGS gives none, only
the farthest time there is. What was read before a seek is dropped. The
model is asked a word as the track opens, so a server that unloads it
when idle has it loaded by the first picture.

The model is asked for the text only, numbered; an answer that is not is
dropped for its batch. When the model refuses three batches in a row, or
answers them unread, it is taken to read no pictures - a provider that
does not answer says nothing of that - and the pictures are shown as
they are. With a track of text in another language the translation still
starts from it; one of pictures is chosen only when there is none.

The codec factory hands such a stream to the new codec: that seam and
the two new files in the build list are all the shared code touched.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
